"""`FullScreen`: an example plugin that replaces Bazel's terminal UI with a
full-screen dashboard of its own, to show what AXL can do to customize
Bazel's behavior. Not a port: BuildBuddy has no plugin like it.

    aspect build //... --full-screen:enabled=true

While a build runs, the terminal's alternate screen shows:

- a header with the task, how long Bazel has run, and a bar of its
  `[done / total]` actions;
- the actions running now, from Bazel's own progress area, oldest first,
  yellow past a minute and red past five;
- links to the invocation's results pages: the Aspect Build Results page the
  CLI streams to, and the BES results page Bazel streams to itself with
  `--bes_results_url`, each when there is one (OSC 8 hyperlinks: Cmd+click);
- the targets and tests the build event stream reported, the share of
  processes the cache served once Bazel tallies them, and a list: the
  Failures (each failed action, which Bazel reports every one of, and each
  test attempt that did not pass, then the failed targets Bazel gave no log
  for), or, with `a`, the actions that succeeded and wrote output (with
  `--full-screen:actions=all`, which has Bazel report them);
- Bazel's console, scrollable, with errors and warnings coloured, counting
  the lines that arrive while it is scrolled up; or, in its place, a
  record's log (an action's stderr and stdout, a test attempt's `test.log`,
  its end when long), found where Bazel left it, a test's in the output tree
  even when a remote cache names it by `bytestream://`;
- a row of the keys for what has focus, as many as fit, and `?` for all of
  them (`_HELP`).

The keys, as in `less`, lazygit and Claude Code's transcript, none needing
one a Mac laptop lacks (Page Up, Home, F-keys, Option): `↑`/`↓` or `k`/`j`
scroll a line, the mouse wheel three (`m` gives the mouse back to the
terminal, to select text, `--full-screen:mouse=false` from the start),
Shift+`↑`/`↓`, `u`/`d` and Ctrl+`u`/`d` half the console's height, `b`/Space
all of it, `g` to the oldest line, `G` back to following; `e` cycles
through every line, errors and warnings, and errors only; Tab moves to the
list and back, where `↑`/`↓` choose and Enter shows a log, and `]`/`[` show
the chosen record's, then step to the next or previous from anywhere; `z`
gives the console the whole screen; Esc closes the innermost of the search
bar, the list's focus and a log; `q` cancels the build (as Ctrl+C does).

Ctrl+F or `/` opens a search bar for a regular expression (`std.re`),
smart case unless Ctrl+T (or Alt+C) makes it match case, whole words with
Ctrl+W (or Alt+W). As you type it jumps to the newest match at or above
the view, marked and centred, every match highlighted; Tab in the bar
filters to the matching lines instead. Enter keeps it and closes the bar,
and `n`/`N` then step to older and newer matches; Ctrl+U clears it; Esc
clears it and goes back to where the bar opened. While it is open every key
types into it but the arrows and the wheel, which still scroll (or, with
the list focused, choose).

When a `build` or `test` task ends the screen stays up, its timer stopped
and the CLI's closing lines in the console, its failures still browsable,
until `q`, Enter or Esc with nothing open to close, or Ctrl+C. With nothing
failed it counts down three seconds and leaves, unless a key stops it
(`--full-screen:stay=always` stays, `never`, or a stdin that is not a
terminal, leaves at once, as does a task cancelled with `q` or Ctrl+C).
Then the scrollback gets what the CLI printed meanwhile, Bazel's errors,
each failure with its log's path, and the path of Bazel's full console log.

How it is built:

1. `BazelTrait.output_display = "none"`: Bazel's console is still captured
   and run through every stage, but nothing of it is shown.
2. A `BazelTrait.output_frame` hook, which the runtime calls about every
   100ms while the console is open, takes the terminal on its first call
   (`ctx.std.term.take()`, and the mouse's wheel with `screen.mouse(True)`),
   then on each call reads what the console would have shown
   (`build.output_feed()`, through `add_lines`), reads the keys
   (`screen.key()`, through `on_key`), and redraws the whole screen in one
   write (`render`).
3. `build_event` hooks count targets and tests from the build event stream,
   gather the failures and the actions that wrote output with their logs
   (`failure_of`, `output_of`), and what finding a test's log in the output
   tree takes (the exec root, each configuration's output folder, each
   test's run and shard counts and last attempts).
4. An `output_end` hook draws the build's last frame. A retry keeps the
   screen, starting afresh on its first frame.
   Any other task (`lint`, `warming`) gives the terminal back there, as it
   goes on after its build.
5. A `post_task` hook keeps the screen up (or counts down, the task's exit
   code 0 and nothing failed), with Ctrl+C read as a key
   (`screen.interrupts(False)`) since the task has already ended, then gives
   the terminal back (`screen.release()`) and prints the summary.

Only on a terminal, and never on CI. Off by default.
"""

load("@aspect//bazel.axl", "BazelTrait")
load("@aspect//helpers.axl", "file_uri_to_path")
load("@aspect//traits.axl", "Phases")
load("@std//time.axl", "monotonic")
load("./open_invocation.axl", "bes_results_url", "results_pages")

_RESET = "\x1b[0m"
_BOLD = "\x1b[1m"
_DIM = "\x1b[2m"
_INVERSE = "\x1b[7m"
_RED = "\x1b[31m"
_GREEN = "\x1b[32m"
_YELLOW = "\x1b[33m"
_CYAN = "\x1b[36m"

# Console lines kept for scrolling back.
_MAX_LOG = 5000

# The most of a failure's log read into its view: the end, where the failure
# usually is.
_MAX_LOG_BYTES = 1024 * 1024

# Escape sequences a log file may hold (colour, cursor moves, OSC links,
# character set choices, two-byte ones such as saving the cursor).
ANSI = "\\x1b\\[[0-?]*[ -/]*[@-~]|\\x1b\\][^\\x07\\x1b]*(?:\\x07|\\x1b\\\\)|\\x1b[ -/]+[0-~]|\\x1b[0-~]"

# Control characters a log line may still hold once its escape sequences are
# gone, C1's 8-bit ones among them, which would move the cursor off the row
# (a tab is spaced by `fit`).
CONTROLS = "[\\x00-\\x08\\x0b-\\x1f\\x7f-\\x9f]"

# The tasks whose screen stays up after their build: the others go on to
# run something that needs the terminal.
_STAY_KINDS = ["build", "test"]

# Errors repeated in the scrollback once the screen goes.
_MAX_ERRORS = 20

# Test statuses that are failures; `no_status`, `incomplete` and the like are
# not.
_FAILED_TEST_STATUSES = ["failed", "timeout", "failed_to_build", "remote_failure", "tool_halted_before_testing"]

# The Aborted reasons a target counts as failed for: the rest (skipped, an
# interrupt) are skipped.
_FAILED_ABORTS = ["analysis_failure", "loading_failure"]

_SPINNER = "⠋⠙⠹⠸⠼⠴⠦⠧⠇⠏"

# What has Bazel report every action, for `actions = "all"`.
_PUBLISH_ALL_ACTIONS = "--build_event_publish_all_actions"

# How many lines a turn of the mouse wheel scrolls.
_WHEEL = 3

# How long a finished screen with nothing failed stays up (`stay =
# "failure"`), counting down.
_COUNTDOWN_MS = 3000

# How old, in seconds, a running action is coloured slow, and stuck.
_SLOW_S = 60
_STUCK_S = 300

# The keys, for the `?` overlay.
_HELP = [
    ("↑ ↓  k j", "scroll a line; in a list, choose"),
    ("⇧↑ ⇧↓  u d  ^U ^D", "scroll half a page"),
    ("Space  b", "scroll a page"),
    ("g  G", "the oldest line; back to following the newest"),
    ("wheel", "scroll (m turns the mouse off, to select text, and on)"),
    ("e", "every line, errors and warnings, errors only"),
    ("/  ^F", "find: jumps to the newest match; then n older, N newer"),
    ("  Tab", "in the bar: filter to the matches instead, and back"),
    ("  ^T  ^W", "in the bar: match case (else a capital does), whole words"),
    ("  ^U  Enter  Esc", "in the bar: clear; keep and close; clear and go back"),
    ("Tab", "the list in the Results pane, and back"),
    ("Enter", "in the list: show the chosen log"),
    ("]  [", "the next or previous log"),
    ("a", "the list: failures, or actions that wrote output"),
    ("z", "the console fills the screen, and back"),
    ("Esc", "close the innermost: the bar, the list, a log"),
    ("q", "cancel the build; once it is done, leave"),
    ("?", "these keys"),
]

def fit(text: str, width: int) -> str:
    """`text` cut or padded with spaces to `width` characters."""
    if width <= 0:
        return ""

    # A tab would move the cursor off the row.
    text = text.replace("\t", "  ")
    if len(text) > width:
        return text[:width - 1] + "…" if width > 1 else text[:width]
    return text + " " * (width - len(text))

def progress_counts(status: str):
    """`(done, total)` from Bazel's status line (`[5,619 / 13,381] …`), or
    None when it has none."""
    if not status.startswith("[") or " / " not in status or "]" not in status:
        return None
    inner = status[1:status.index("]")]
    done, _, total = inner.partition(" / ")
    done = done.replace(",", "").strip()
    total = total.replace(",", "").strip()
    if not done.isdigit() or not total.isdigit():
        return None
    return (int(done), int(total))

def running_actions(progress: list) -> list:
    """The running actions in Bazel's progress area: the rows under its
    status line, or, when there are none, the one action a status line names
    itself (`[n / m] Compiling a.cc; 3s …`), as Bazel draws a single action."""
    rows = [r.strip() for r in progress[1:] if r.strip()]
    if rows or not progress:
        return rows
    status = progress[0]
    if not status.startswith("[") or "] " not in status:
        return []
    rest = status.partition("] ")[2].strip()
    words = rest.split(" ")
    if not rest or rest.startswith("no action") or (len(words) > 1 and words[0].isdigit() and words[1].startswith("action")):
        return []
    return [rest]

def _count(word: str) -> int:
    """`word` as a count, its thousands separators (`1,204`) and a trailing
    comma dropped; -1 for a word that is none."""
    digits = word.rstrip(",").replace(",", "")
    return int(digits) if digits.isdigit() else -1

def action_counts(progress: list):
    """`(running, started)` from Bazel's status line: `[n / m] 300 actions
    running` (300 of each), or `[n / m] 400 actions, 300 running` with some
    started but not yet running (remote execution, a full local pool); -1 for
    what the line does not name. Bazel lists only `--ui_actions_shown` of the
    running ones under it, 8 by default."""
    if not progress or "] " not in progress[0]:
        return (-1, -1)
    words = progress[0].partition("] ")[2].split()
    running, started = -1, -1
    for i in range(len(words) - 1):
        n = _count(words[i])
        if n < 0:
            continue
        after = words[i + 1].rstrip(",")
        if after.startswith("action"):
            started = n
            if i + 2 < len(words) and words[i + 2] == "running":
                running = n
        elif after == "running":
            running = n
    return (running, started)

def bar(done: int, total: int, width: int) -> str:
    """A `width`-character bar, filled by `done` of `total`."""
    if width <= 0 or total <= 0:
        return ""
    filled = min(width, width * done // total)
    return "█" * filled + "░" * (width - filled)

# What `e` cycles the console through, in order.
_FILTERS = ["all", "warnings", "errors"]

_FILTER_TITLES = {"all": "", "warnings": " (errors and warnings)", "errors": " (errors only)"}

_REVERSE = "\x1b[7m"
_NO_REVERSE = "\x1b[27m"

def new_search() -> dict:
    """The search bar's state: closed, empty, smart case, jumping to matches
    (`mode`, or `filter` to show only them). `origin` is where the console
    was scrolled when the bar opened, `anchored` when that was not following
    the newest line (scrolled up, or held on a match), and `back` the current
    match, as lines from the newest shown (-1 for none): both counted from
    the end, so new lines arriving keep them on the same line."""
    return {"open": False, "query": "", "case": False, "word": False, "pattern": None, "error": "", "mode": "jump", "origin": 0, "anchored": False, "back": -1}

def has_capital(query: str) -> bool:
    """Whether `query` has a capital letter of its own: not one naming an
    escape (`\\W`, `\\pL`, `\\p{Lu}`), a hex digit of one (`\\x4A`,
    `\\u{00C9}`, `\\U0001F600`), a flag (`(?U)`, `(?iR:…)`) or a group
    (`(?P<Name>…)`, `(?<Name>…)`)."""
    at = ""
    end = ""
    need = 0
    full = 0
    recent = ""
    for c in query.elems():
        prev = recent
        recent = (recent + c)[-3:]
        if at == "skip":
            if c == end:
                at = ""
            continue
        if at == "flags":
            if c in "imsxURu-":
                continue
            at = ""
            if c in "):":
                continue
        if at == "hex":
            if c == "{" and need == full:
                at = "skip"
                end = "}"
                continue
            if need > 0 and c in "0123456789abcdefABCDEF":
                need -= 1
                if need == 0:
                    at = ""
                continue
            at = ""
        if at == "escape":
            # An escaped character opens nothing: `\\(?U` is no flag group.
            at = ""
            recent = ""
            if c in ("p", "P"):
                at = "class"
            elif c in ("x", "u", "U"):
                at = "hex"
                need = {"x": 2, "u": 4, "U": 8}[c]
                full = need
            continue
        if at == "class":
            at = ""
            if c == "{":
                at = "skip"
                end = "}"
            continue
        if c == "\\":
            at = "escape"
            continue
        if prev.endswith("(?") or prev.endswith("(?P"):
            if c == "<":
                at = "skip"
                end = ">"
                continue
            if c == "P" and prev.endswith("(?"):
                continue
            if c in "imsxURu-" and prev.endswith("(?"):
                at = "flags"
                continue
        if c != c.lower():
            return True
    return False

def case_sensitive(search: dict) -> bool:
    """Whether the search matches case: when Ctrl+T says so, else, smart case
    as in fzf and ripgrep, when its query has a capital (`has_capital`)."""
    return search["case"] or has_capital(search["query"])

def _search_update(state: dict):
    """Recompile the search's pattern from its query and toggles. A query
    that does not compile (one still being typed) keeps the last pattern that
    did, its error shown in the bar."""
    search = state["search"]
    if not search["query"]:
        search["pattern"] = None
        search["error"] = ""
        return
    case = case_sensitive(search)
    error = state["re"].check(search["query"], case_sensitive = case, whole_word = search["word"])
    if error != None:
        search["error"] = error
        return
    search["error"] = ""
    search["pattern"] = state["re"].compile(search["query"], case_sensitive = case, whole_word = search["word"])

def _search_key(state: dict, key: str, page: int) -> bool:
    """Apply `key` to an open search bar; False for a key it leaves to the
    console (the arrows and the wheel, which scroll). A change finds anew:
    jumping, the newest match at or above where the bar opened (`_find`);
    filtering, the newest line, or with no query where the bar opened."""
    search = state["search"]
    if key == "enter":
        search["open"] = False
        return True
    if key == "esc":
        state["search"] = new_search()
        state["scroll"] = search["origin"]
        return True
    if key == "tab":
        search["mode"] = "filter" if search["mode"] == "jump" else "jump"
        search["back"] = -1
    elif key in ("ctrl-t", "alt-c"):
        search["case"] = not search["case"]
    elif key in ("ctrl-w", "alt-w"):
        search["word"] = not search["word"]
    elif key == "ctrl-u":
        search["query"] = ""
    elif key == "backspace":
        search["query"] = search["query"][:-1]
    elif key.startswith("paste:"):
        search["query"] += paste_text(key)
    elif len(key) == 1:
        search["query"] += key
    else:
        return key not in ("up", "down", "shift-up", "shift-down", "wheel-up", "wheel-down")
    _search_update(state)
    if search["mode"] == "jump":
        _find(state, page)
    else:
        # Filtering shows the newest match; with no query, where it opened.
        state["scroll"] = 0 if search["pattern"] != None else search["origin"]
    return True

def shown_lines(state: dict) -> list:
    """What the console pane lists: a log's lines or the console's, through
    the `e` filter and a filtering search (`console_lines`)."""
    view = state["view"]
    return console_lines(state, view["lines"] if view != None else state.get("log", []))

def _match_from(pattern, lines: list, start: int, step: int) -> int:
    """The first of `lines` from `start` on, going `step` (-1 older, 1
    newer), that `pattern` matches; -1 for none."""
    i = start
    for _ in range(len(lines)):
        if i < 0 or i >= len(lines):
            break
        if pattern.search(lines[i]):
            return i
        i += step
    return -1

def _show_match(state: dict, lines: list, i: int, page: int):
    """Make line `i` of `lines` the current match, scrolled to the middle of
    a console `page` lines high."""
    state["search"]["back"] = len(lines) - 1 - i
    state["scroll"] = max(0, len(lines) - i - 1 - (page - 1) // 2)

def _find(state: dict, page: int):
    """Jump to the newest match at or above the bottom of the console as the
    bar found it (its `origin`), else the newest of all; with none, or no
    query, back to that `origin`. A query that does not compile moves
    nothing."""
    search = state["search"]
    if search["error"]:
        return
    lines = shown_lines(state)
    at = -1
    if search["pattern"] != None:
        at = _match_from(search["pattern"], lines, len(lines) - 1 - search["origin"], -1)
        if at < 0:
            at = _match_from(search["pattern"], lines, len(lines) - 1, -1)
    if at < 0:
        search["back"] = -1
        state["scroll"] = search["origin"]
        return
    _show_match(state, lines, at, page)

def step_match(state: dict, step: int, page: int):
    """`n` (`step` -1) and `N` (1): the next older or newer match from the
    current one, or from the bottom of the console without one; nothing
    past the oldest or the newest."""
    search = state["search"]
    if search["pattern"] == None or search["mode"] != "jump":
        return
    lines = shown_lines(state)
    back = search["back"]
    if back >= 0 and back < len(lines):
        start = len(lines) - 1 - back + step
    else:
        # The bottom of the console as `render` draws it, a scroll past the
        # top (`g`) at the top page.
        bottom = max(min(len(lines) - state["scroll"], len(lines)), min(len(lines), page)) - 1
        start = bottom + (1 if step > 0 else 0)
    at = _match_from(search["pattern"], lines, start, step)
    if at >= 0:
        _show_match(state, lines, at, page)

def paste_text(key: str) -> str:
    """A `paste:` key's text for the search bar: on one line, without control
    characters, which would move the cursor or count as columns they are not."""
    out = []
    for c in key[len("paste:"):].elems():
        if c in "\r\n\t":
            out.append(" ")
        elif c >= " " and not ("\x7f" <= c and c <= "\x9f"):
            out.append(c)
    return "".join(out)

def failure_of(kind: str, event_id, payload):
    """A record for the Failures list from a build event, or None: a failed
    action (`action_completed`, which Bazel sends for every failure) with its
    stderr and stdout, or a test attempt that did not pass (`test_result`;
    flaky ones did not) with its `test.log`. Each log is a BES `File.file`: a
    URI, or the contents inline."""
    label = str(_get(event_id, "label", "") or "")
    if kind == "action_completed":
        if _get(payload, "success", True):
            return None
        return _action_record(label, payload)
    if kind == "test_result":
        if str(_get(payload, "status", "") or "").lower() not in _FAILED_TEST_STATUSES:
            return None
        attempt = _get(event_id, "attempt", 1) or 1
        return {
            "kind": "test",
            "label": label,
            "detail": "attempt %d" % attempt,
            "run": _get(event_id, "run", 1) or 1,
            "shard": _get(event_id, "shard", 1) or 1,
            "attempt": attempt,
            "config": str(_get(_get(event_id, "configuration", None), "id", "") or ""),
            "files": [("test.log", bounded_log(_get(f, "file", None))) for f in (_get(payload, "test_action_output", None) or []) if str(_get(f, "name", "") or "") == "test.log"],
        }
    return None

def _action_record(label: str, payload) -> dict:
    """An action's record for a list: its label, mnemonic and logs."""
    files = []
    for name in ("stderr", "stdout"):
        f = _get(payload, name, None)
        if f != None:
            files.append((name, bounded_log(_get(f, "file", None))))
    return {"kind": "action", "label": label, "detail": str(_get(payload, "type", "") or "") or "Action", "files": files}

def output_of(kind: str, event_id, payload):
    """A record for the actions list from a build event, or None: an action
    that succeeded and wrote a stdout or stderr, which Bazel reports only
    with `--build_event_publish_all_actions` (`actions = "all"`)."""
    if kind != "action_completed" or not _get(payload, "success", False):
        return None
    record = _action_record(str(_get(event_id, "label", "") or ""), payload)
    return record if record["files"] else None

def cut_text(text: str) -> str:
    """An inline log's end, cut anywhere, the byte before the cut first (see
    `bounded_log`), started where `std.fs.try_read_tail` starts a file's: at
    the cut when it falls at a line's start, else the next line when one
    follows, else the first whole character."""
    if text.startswith("\n"):
        return text[1:]
    head, newline, rest = text.partition("\n")
    return rest if newline and rest else text[1:].lstrip("\ufffd")

def bounded_log(value, cap = _MAX_LOG_BYTES):
    """A log's BES `File.file`: a URI as it is, or inline contents as
    `(bytes, cut)`, cut to their last `cap` bytes and the one before (to see
    whether the cut falls at a line's start, as `cut_text` reads it), so the
    rest of a failed action's tens of MB never stays on the heap."""
    if type(value) == "bytes" and len(value) > cap:
        return (value[-(cap + 1):], True)
    if type(value) == "bytes":
        return (value, False)
    return value

def local_test_logs(state: dict, record: dict) -> list:
    """Where Bazel's output tree has a test attempt's `test.log`, for one a
    remote cache named by `bytestream://`: `<exec root>/bazel-out/<output
    dir>/testlogs/[external/<repo>/]<package>/<name>/[shard_<s>_of_<n>][_]
    [run_<r>_of_<m>]/`, `test_attempts/attempt_<a>.log` for an attempt
    Bazel retried, `test.log` for its last. Both the folder (its shard and
    run counts) and which attempt was last come from the test's summary,
    sent after its last attempt: before it, no path, rather than a guess
    that could name an older build's log."""
    root = state["exec_root"]
    out = state["output_dirs"].get(record["config"], "")
    label = record["label"].lstrip("@")
    key = (record["label"], record["config"])
    if not root or not out or key not in state["test_counts"] or "//" not in label or ":" not in label.partition("//")[2]:
        return []
    repo, _, rest = label.partition("//")
    package, _, name = rest.partition(":")
    path = root + "/bazel-out/" + out + "/testlogs" + ("/external/" + repo if repo else "")
    for part in (package + "/" + name).split("/"):
        if part:
            path += "/" + part
    runs, shards = state["test_counts"][key]
    run, shard, attempt = record["run"], record["shard"], record["attempt"]
    sub = (["shard_%d_of_%d" % (shard, shards)] if shards > 1 else []) + (["run_%d_of_%d" % (run, runs)] if runs > 1 else [])
    if sub:
        path += "/" + "_".join(sub)
    last = state["last_attempts"].get((record["label"], record["config"], run, shard), attempt)
    return [path + ("/test_attempts/attempt_%d.log" % attempt if attempt < last else "/test.log")]

def log_paths(state: dict, record: dict, uri) -> list:
    """Where a log named by `uri` may be on disk, likeliest first."""
    if type(uri) != "string" or not uri:
        return []
    paths = []
    if uri.startswith("bytestream://") and record["kind"] == "test":
        paths.extend(local_test_logs(state, record))
    paths.append(file_uri_to_path(state["env"], uri))
    return [p for p in paths if p]

def log_view(state: dict, record: dict) -> dict:
    """The view of a failure's logs: its title, the lines (the end of each
    log, escape sequences stripped, a divider between an action's stderr and
    stdout) and whether any was cut. The logs share `_MAX_LOG` lines (`fit_logs`)."""
    blocks = []
    cut = False
    for name, value in record["files"]:
        text = None
        if type(value) == "tuple":
            contents, inline_cut = value
            text = str(contents)
            cut = cut or inline_cut
            if inline_cut:
                text = cut_text(text)
        for path in log_paths(state, record, value):
            got = state["read"](path, _MAX_LOG_BYTES)
            if got != None:
                text, skipped = got
                cut = cut or skipped > 0
                break
        if text == None:
            pending = record["kind"] == "test" and (record["label"], record["config"]) not in state["test_counts"]
            if pending and type(value) == "string" and value.startswith("bytestream://"):
                blocks.append((name, ["(%s is found once the test has finished: a remote cache names it)" % name]))
            else:
                blocks.append((name, ["(%s is not on this machine: only in the remote cache)" % name]))
            continue
        if not text.strip():
            blocks.append((name, ["(empty)"]))
            continue

        # A line's last carriage return wins, as on a terminal (a progress
        # bar redrawing itself, a CRLF line end); no control is left to move
        # the cursor off its row.
        own = []
        for line in state["ansi"].replace(text, "").rstrip("\n").split("\n"):
            line = line.rstrip("\r").rpartition("\r")[2]
            own.append(state["controls"].replace(line, ""))
        blocks.append((name, own))
    kept = fit_logs([own for _, own in blocks], _MAX_LOG)
    lines = []
    for (name, own), keep in zip(blocks, kept):
        if len(blocks) > 1:
            lines.append("── " + name + " ──")
        if keep < len(own):
            cut = True
        lines.extend(own[len(own) - keep:])
    if not record["files"]:
        lines.append("(Bazel reported no log for it)")
    return {"title": record["label"] + " · " + failure_detail(state, record), "lines": lines, "cut": cut, "record": record}

def failure_detail(state: dict, record: dict) -> str:
    """What tells a failure apart from others of its label: an action's
    mnemonic; a test attempt's number, after its shard and run when the
    test has (its summary's counts) or has been seen to have (`spread`, the
    highest of each its results named) more than one."""
    if record["kind"] != "test":
        return record["detail"]
    key = (record["label"], record["config"])
    runs, shards = state["test_counts"].get(key, (0, 0))
    seen_runs, seen_shards = state["spread"].get(key, (1, 1))
    parts = []
    if max(shards, seen_shards) > 1:
        parts.append("shard %d" % record["shard"])
    if max(runs, seen_runs) > 1:
        parts.append("run %d" % record["run"])
    return " · ".join(parts + [record["detail"]])

def fit_logs(logs: list, room: int) -> list:
    """How many of each log's last lines to keep for `room` lines in all:
    every log whole when they fit, else each over a fair share cut to it,
    the share of a log under it passed to the others."""
    keep = [len(log) for log in logs]
    total = 0
    for n in keep:
        total += n
    if total <= room:
        return keep
    left = room
    order = sorted(range(len(logs)), key = lambda i: keep[i])
    for n, i in enumerate(order):
        share = left // (len(order) - n)
        keep[i] = min(keep[i], share)
        left -= keep[i]
    return keep

def reset_attempt(state: dict, now_ms: int):
    """Start a retried attempt afresh: its own counts, console, failures and
    actions, no log open nor the list focused, timed from `now_ms`. The
    search (but its current match and where it opened), the `e` filter, the
    list shown, zoom and the mouse are the user's, kept."""
    state["search"]["back"] = -1
    state["search"]["origin"] = 0
    state["search"]["anchored"] = False
    state.update(
        fresh = False,
        phase = "Starting Bazel…",
        fails = [],
        outputs = [],
        selected = 0,
        picked = 0,
        cache = None,
        unseen = 0,
        focus = "console",
        view = None,
        last_attempts = {},
        spread = {},
        logged_index = None,
        test_counts = {},
        counts = new_counts(),
        errors = [],
        error_count = 0,
        log = [],
        scroll = 0,
        start_ms = now_ms,
        bazel_uuid = "",
    )

def note_event(state: dict, kind: str, event_id, payload, aborted: bool):
    """Gather from one build event what the Failures list needs: a failure's
    record (`failure_of`), and what finding a test's log in the output tree
    takes (the exec root, each configuration's output folder, each test's
    run and shard counts, each run and shard's last attempt, and the most
    runs and shards it has been seen to have, `spread`). A summary
    the build announced but did not get to (`aborted`, an interrupt) has no
    counts. A test's summary arriving shows its open log anew, now
    findable, from its end."""
    if aborted:
        return
    if kind == "workspace_config":
        state["exec_root"] = str(_get(payload, "local_exec_root", "") or "")
    elif kind == "configuration":
        state["output_dirs"][str(_get(event_id, "id", "") or "")] = str(_get(payload, "mnemonic", "") or "")
    elif kind == "test_summary":
        key = (str(_get(event_id, "label", "") or ""), str(_get(_get(event_id, "configuration", None), "id", "") or ""))
        state["test_counts"][key] = (_get(payload, "run_count", 0) or 0, _get(payload, "shard_count", 0) or 0)
        view = state["view"]
        if view != None and view["record"]["kind"] == "test" and (view["record"]["label"], view["record"]["config"]) == key:
            state["view"] = log_view(state, view["record"])
            state["scroll"] = 0
            state["search"]["back"] = -1
    elif kind == "test_result":
        key = (
            str(_get(event_id, "label", "") or ""),
            str(_get(_get(event_id, "configuration", None), "id", "") or ""),
            _get(event_id, "run", 1) or 1,
            _get(event_id, "shard", 1) or 1,
        )
        state["last_attempts"][key] = max(state["last_attempts"].get(key, 0), _get(event_id, "attempt", 1) or 1)
        runs, shards = state["spread"].get(key[:2], (1, 1))
        state["spread"][key[:2]] = (max(runs, key[2]), max(shards, key[3]))
    record = failure_of(kind, event_id, payload)
    if record != None:
        state["fails"].append(record)
    record = output_of(kind, event_id, payload)
    if record != None:
        state["outputs"].append(record)

def _items(state: dict) -> list:
    """The records of the list the Results pane shows: the failures, or,
    after `a`, the actions that wrote output."""
    return state.get("outputs", []) if state.get("list") == "actions" else state["fails"]

def _pick(state: dict) -> str:
    """The key of `state` that holds the chosen row of the list shown: each
    list keeps its own."""
    return "picked" if state.get("list") == "actions" else "selected"

def _open_failure(state: dict, index: int):
    """Show the record at `index` of the list shown in the console pane, at
    its end."""
    state[_pick(state)] = index
    state["view"] = log_view(state, _items(state)[index])
    state["scroll"] = 0
    state["search"]["back"] = -1
    state["focus"] = "console"

def logged_index(state: dict) -> dict:
    """The labels with a log in the Failures list (`logged`), how many of
    them are also among the failed targets (`both`), and the failed targets
    without one in their order (`unlogged`, some of which may have gained a
    log since, skipped), caught up with just the records and labels added
    since the last call: so a build's tens of thousands of failures cost
    each frame only what is new."""
    index = state.get("logged_index")
    fails = state["fails"]
    failed = state["counts"]["failures"]

    # Lists replaced (a retry's) without the index cleared: start over.
    if index != None and (index["fails"] > len(fails) or index["failures"] > len(failed)):
        index = None
    if index == None:
        index = {"logged": {}, "listed": {}, "both": 0, "fails": 0, "failures": 0, "unlogged": [], "dropped": 0, "head": 0}
        state["logged_index"] = index
    for rec in fails[index["fails"]:]:
        label = rec["label"]
        if label not in index["logged"]:
            index["logged"][label] = True
            if label in index["listed"]:
                index["both"] += 1
                index["dropped"] += 1
    for label in failed[index["failures"]:]:
        if label not in index["listed"]:
            index["listed"][label] = True
            if label in index["logged"]:
                index["both"] += 1
            else:
                index["unlogged"].append(label)

    # The labels that gained a log stay in `unlogged` until half of it is
    # them (or a walk skips more than a few past its head, `first_unlogged`),
    # then go.
    if index["dropped"] * 2 > len(index["unlogged"]):
        _compact(index)
    index["fails"] = len(fails)
    index["failures"] = len(failed)
    return index

def _compact(index: dict):
    """Drop from `unlogged` the labels that have gained a log."""
    index["unlogged"] = [label for label in index["unlogged"] if label not in index["logged"]]
    index["dropped"] = 0
    index["head"] = 0

def first_unlogged(index: dict, wanted: int) -> list:
    """The first `wanted` failed targets without a log, in order. A head
    kept past the leading ones that have gained a log (which never lose it)
    only moves forward, and more than a few skipped further in compacts the
    list, so each frame's walk is bounded and every skip is paid for once."""
    unlogged = index["unlogged"]

    # By index, never a slice: a slice would copy the whole list each frame.
    head = index.get("head", 0)
    for i in range(head, len(unlogged)):
        if unlogged[i] not in index["logged"]:
            break
        head = i + 1
    index["head"] = head
    found = []
    skipped = 0
    for i in range(head, len(unlogged)):
        label = unlogged[i]
        if label in index["logged"]:
            skipped += 1
            continue
        found.append(label)
        if len(found) == wanted:
            break
    if skipped > 64:
        _compact(index)
    return found

def failure_rows(state: dict, room: int, width: int) -> list:
    """The Failures list's rows, `room` at most, each `width` wide: a ✗ and
    the label with its mnemonic or attempt, the selected one in reverse
    video while the list has focus, kept in sight, with how many are hidden
    above and below; then the failed targets Bazel reported no log for. Only
    the rows shown are drawn, and the counts come from `logged_index`, kept
    up as failures arrive, so a frame never walks them all."""
    fails = state["fails"]
    failed = state["counts"]["failures"]
    index = logged_index(state)
    logged = index["logged"]
    total = len(fails) + len(failed) - index["both"]
    if room <= 0 or total == 0:
        return []
    selected = state["selected"]
    start, shown, above, below = _window(total, room, selected)

    # Just the window's unlogged ones. They follow the logged rows, and the
    # window starts at or before the chosen (logged) row, so they are the
    # first few of the index's.
    wanted = start + shown - len(fails)
    unlogged = first_unlogged(index, wanted) if wanted > 0 else []

    def row(i):
        if i >= len(fails):
            return _DIM + fit("✗ " + unlogged[i - len(fails)], width).rstrip(" ") + _RESET
        rec = fails[i]
        text = fit("✗ " + rec["label"] + "  " + failure_detail(state, rec), width).rstrip(" ")
        if i == selected and state["focus"] == "failures":
            return _REVERSE + fit(text, width) + _NO_REVERSE
        return _RED + "✗" + _RESET + text[1:]

    rows = [row(i) for i in range(start, start + shown) if i < len(fails) or i - len(fails) < len(unlogged)]
    return _counted(rows, above, below)

def _window(total: int, room: int, selected: int):
    """Which of a list's `total` rows `room` lines show: `(start, shown,
    above, below)`, a window around the `selected` row, a line on each side
    counting what is hidden there (`above`, `below`); one with nothing to
    count gives its line back."""
    if total <= room:
        return (0, total, 0, 0)
    if room < 3:
        return (min(max(0, selected - room + 1), total - room), room, 0, 0)
    shown = room - 2
    start = min(max(0, selected - shown // 2), total - shown)
    if start == 0:
        shown += 1
    elif start + shown >= total:
        start -= 1
        shown += 1
    return (start, shown, start, total - start - shown)

def _counted(rows: list, above: int, below: int) -> list:
    """A list's `rows` with how many are hidden above and below them."""
    return ([_DIM + "… %d earlier" % above + _RESET] if above else []) + rows + ([_DIM + "… %d more" % below + _RESET] if below else [])

def action_rows(state: dict, room: int, width: int) -> list:
    """The actions list's rows, `room` at most, each `width` wide: a • and
    the label with its mnemonic, the chosen one in reverse video while the
    list has focus, kept in sight."""
    outputs = state.get("outputs", [])
    if room <= 0 or not outputs:
        return []
    picked = state.get("picked", 0)
    start, shown, above, below = _window(len(outputs), room, picked)
    rows = []
    for i in range(start, start + shown):
        rec = outputs[i]
        text = fit("• " + rec["label"] + "  " + rec["detail"], width).rstrip(" ")
        if i == picked and state["focus"] == "failures":
            rows.append(_REVERSE + fit(text, width) + _NO_REVERSE)
        else:
            rows.append(_CYAN + "•" + _RESET + text[1:])
    return _counted(rows, above, below)

def console_lines(state: dict, log: list) -> list:
    """The console's lines `state` shows: by its `e` filter, then its search
    when that filters (a search that jumps shows every line)."""
    if state["filter"] != "all":
        log = [l for l in log if shown_by(state["filter"], l)]
    search = state["search"]
    return search["pattern"].filter(log) if search["pattern"] != None and search["mode"] == "filter" else log

def _console_row(pattern):
    """How a console row is coloured: by its severity, then its search
    matches in reverse video (found in the plain text, so a match never
    lands inside a colour's escape sequence)."""

    def colour(body):
        if pattern == None:
            return _colour(body)
        kind = severity(body)
        marked = pattern.highlight(body, _REVERSE, _NO_REVERSE)
        if kind == "error":
            return _RED + marked + _RESET
        if kind == "warning":
            return _YELLOW + marked + _RESET
        return marked

    return colour

def _search_bar(search: dict, matches: int, cols: int) -> str:
    """The bar's row, `cols` wide at most: whether it finds or filters, the
    query, its toggles lit when on (case when it matches case, by Ctrl+T or
    a capital), what it found (nothing for an empty query) or what is wrong
    with it, and its keys while they fit (the query's start goes before the
    toggles do)."""
    found = search["error"] or ("%d match%s" % (matches, "" if matches == 1 else "es") if search["query"] else "")
    jump = search["mode"] == "jump"
    hints = " · Tab %s · ^T case · ^W word · Enter keep · Esc back" % ("filter" if jump else "find")
    toggles = " Aa W "
    head = " Find: " if jump else " Filter: "

    # Measured as plain text, the escape sequences added after.
    room = cols - len(toggles) - len(found) - 1
    if room - len(hints) >= len(head) + len(search["query"]) + 1:
        room -= len(hints)
    else:
        hints = ""
    query = search["query"]
    if len(head) + len(query) + 1 > room:
        head = " /"
        keep = max(0, room - len(head) - 2)
        if len(query) > keep + 1:
            query = ("…" + query[len(query) - keep:]) if keep > 0 else ""
    if len(head) + len(query) + 1 > room:
        return fit(" /" + search["query"], cols)

    def toggle(on, label):
        return (_REVERSE if on else _DIM) + label + _RESET

    return (
        head + query + "▏" + " " + toggle(case_sensitive(search), "Aa") + " " + toggle(search["word"], "W") + " " +
        (_RED + found + _RESET if search["error"] else found) + _DIM + hints + _RESET
    )

def shown_by(filter: str, line: str) -> bool:
    """Whether the console's `filter` (one of `_FILTERS`) shows `line`."""
    if filter == "all":
        return True
    kind = severity(line)
    return kind == "error" or (kind == "warning" and filter == "warnings")

def severity(line: str) -> str:
    """`error`, `warning` or `` for a console line."""
    if line.startswith("ERROR:") or line.startswith("FAIL:") or " FAILED " in line:
        return "error"
    if line.startswith("WARNING:"):
        return "warning"
    return ""

def elapsed(ms: int) -> str:
    """`ms` as `1m02s` or `12s`."""
    s = ms // 1000
    if s < 60:
        return "%ds" % s
    sec = s % 60
    return "%dm%s%ds" % (s // 60, "0" if sec < 10 else "", sec)

def _get(obj, name: str, default = None):
    """`obj.name`, or `default` when `obj` is None or has no such field."""
    return getattr(obj, name, default) if obj != None else default

def new_counts() -> dict:
    """The tallies `count_event` keeps for one Bazel attempt."""
    return {
        "configured": 0,
        "built": 0,
        "failed": 0,
        "skipped": 0,
        "failures": [],
        "failed_labels": {},
        "aborted": {},
        "tests_passed": 0,
        "tests_failed": 0,
    }

def _fail(counts: dict, label: str):
    """Count a failure, listing its target once however many there are (one
    per configuration, say)."""
    counts["failed"] += 1
    _list_failure(counts, label)

def _list_failure(counts: dict, label: str):
    """List `label` as failed, once: a dict beside the list keeps it O(1)."""
    if label not in counts["failed_labels"]:
        counts["failed_labels"][label] = True
        counts["failures"].append(label)

def count_aborted(counts: dict, label: str, reason: str):
    """Tally a target that did not build, its Aborted `reason` given: failed
    when its loading or analysis did (`_FAILED_ABORTS`), else skipped; once
    per label, as Bazel can report one under two ids."""
    if not label or label in counts["aborted"]:
        return
    counts["aborted"][label] = True
    if reason.lower() in _FAILED_ABORTS:
        _fail(counts, label)
    else:
        counts["skipped"] += 1

def count_event(counts: dict, kind: str, event_id, payload):
    """Tally one build event into `counts`. A target that failed to build
    arrives as `target_completed` without `success`; one that never got that
    far (failed in loading or analysis, skipped, interrupted) as an Aborted
    payload under its own id (`count_aborted`). An aspect's own completion is
    not a target. A test's `overall_status` is lowercase (`passed`, `flaky`,
    `failed`, …); only `_FAILED_TEST_STATUSES` count as failed. A label is
    listed in `failures` once."""
    label = str(_get(event_id, "label", "") or "")

    # A target's own Aborted payload, under its `target_configured` or
    # `target_completed` id; under `configured_label` or `unconfigured_label`
    # it names a root cause (a dependency), not a target asked for.
    if type(payload) == "aborted":
        if kind in ("target_configured", "target_completed") and not _get(event_id, "aspect", ""):
            count_aborted(counts, label, str(_get(payload, "reason", "") or ""))
        return
    if kind == "target_configured":
        if not _get(event_id, "aspect", ""):
            counts["configured"] += 1
    elif kind == "target_completed":
        if _get(event_id, "aspect", ""):
            return
        if _get(payload, "success", False):
            counts["built"] += 1
        else:
            _fail(counts, label)
    elif kind == "test_summary":
        status = str(_get(payload, "overall_status", "") or "")
        if status in ("passed", "flaky"):
            counts["tests_passed"] += 1
        elif status in _FAILED_TEST_STATUSES:
            counts["tests_failed"] += 1
            _list_failure(counts, label)

def _colour(line: str) -> str:
    kind = severity(line)
    if kind == "error":
        return _RED + line + _RESET
    if kind == "warning":
        return _YELLOW + line + _RESET
    if line.startswith("INFO:"):
        return _GREEN + "INFO:" + _RESET + line[len("INFO:"):]
    return line

def link(url: str, text: str) -> str:
    """`text` as an OSC 8 hyperlink to `url`, which a terminal that knows the
    sequence opens on a click and any other shows as `text`."""
    return "\x1b]8;;" + url + "\x1b\\" + text + "\x1b]8;;\x1b\\"

def links_row(pages: list, cols: int) -> str:
    """One row of `(label, url)` results pages, each cut to its share of the
    width but linked to its whole url; `""` without pages."""
    if not pages:
        return ""
    lead = " "
    room = max(0, cols - len(lead) - 3 * (len(pages) - 1))
    each = room // len(pages)
    parts = []
    for label, url in pages:
        shown = fit(label + ": " + url, each).rstrip(" ")
        parts.append(_CYAN + link(url, shown) + _RESET)
    return lead + " · ".join(parts)

def _box_top(title: str, width: int) -> str:
    title = fit(title, width - 5).rstrip(" ")
    return _DIM + "┌─ " + _RESET + _BOLD + title + _RESET + _DIM + " " + "─" * max(0, width - len(title) - 5) + "┐" + _RESET

def _box_row(text: str, width: int, coloured = None, mark = False) -> str:
    """A row of a box `width` wide, `coloured` (a function of its text) when
    given, `mark`ed in its left margin (the current match)."""
    body = fit(text, width - 4)
    if coloured != None:
        # Coloured before the padding, so a highlight never lights it.
        shown = body.rstrip(" ")
        body = coloured(shown) + " " * (len(body) - len(shown))
    left = _DIM + "│" + _RESET + (_BOLD + _YELLOW + "›" + _RESET if mark else " ")
    return left + body + _DIM + " │" + _RESET

def _box_bottom(width: int) -> str:
    return _DIM + "└" + "─" * max(0, width - 2) + "┘" + _RESET

def action_seconds(row: str) -> int:
    """How long a running action has run, in whole seconds, from the time
    Bazel's progress row gives it (`Compiling a.cc; 12s remote`, `…; 1m 30s
    local`); 0 without one."""
    if "; " not in row:
        return 0
    total = 0
    for word in row.rpartition("; ")[2].split(" "):
        unit = word[-1:]
        number = word[:-1]
        if unit not in ("h", "m", "s") or not number or not number.replace(".", "", 1).isdigit():
            break
        total += int(number.partition(".")[0] or "0") * {"h": 3600, "m": 60, "s": 1}[unit]
    return total

def _aged(row: str):
    """How a running action's row is coloured by its age: none, slow, stuck."""
    seconds = action_seconds(row)
    if seconds >= _STUCK_S:
        return lambda text: _RED + text + _RESET
    if seconds >= _SLOW_S:
        return lambda text: _YELLOW + text + _RESET
    return None

def cache_hits(line: str):
    """`(hits, processes)` from Bazel's closing tally (`INFO: 1,204
    processes: 1,000 remote cache hit, 200 internal, 4 darwin-sandbox.`):
    the cache hits of every kind, of the processes not internal to Bazel;
    None for any other line."""
    if not line.startswith("INFO: "):
        return None
    for sep in (" processes: ", " process: "):
        head, found, rest = line[len("INFO: "):].partition(sep)
        if found:
            break
    total = head.replace(",", "")
    if not found or not total.isdigit():
        return None
    hits = 0
    internal = 0
    for part in rest.rstrip(".").split(", "):
        n, _, what = part.partition(" ")
        n = n.replace(",", "")
        if not n.isdigit():
            return None
        if what.endswith("cache hit"):
            hits += int(n)
        elif what == "internal":
            internal += int(n)
    return (hits, int(total) - internal)

def _plain(s: str) -> str:
    """`s` without the escape sequences the Results pane colours with."""
    for code in (_GREEN, _RED, _YELLOW, _CYAN, _RESET, _DIM, _REVERSE, _NO_REVERSE):
        s = s.replace(code, "")
    return s

def _top(state: dict, cols: int, rows: int) -> list:
    """The rows above the console: the header and the progress bar, then,
    unless zoomed (`z`) or showing the keys (`?`), the links, and the running actions, oldest first
    and coloured by age, beside the Results pane, the list (failures or
    actions) under its counts."""
    out = []

    # Header.
    spin = _SPINNER[state["frames"] % len(_SPINNER)] if not state["ended"] else "✔"
    title = " %s aspect %s · %s" % (spin, state["task"], elapsed(state["now_ms"] - state["start_ms"]))
    status = state["progress"][0] if state["progress"] else state["phase"]
    right = " " + status + " "
    out.append(_INVERSE + _BOLD + fit(title, max(0, cols - len(right))) + _RESET + _INVERSE + fit(right, min(len(right), cols)) + _RESET)
    counts = progress_counts(status)
    if counts:
        done, total = counts
        label = fit(" %d / %d actions " % (done, total), cols)
        out.append(_CYAN + bar(done, total, max(0, cols - len(label))) + _RESET + label)
    else:
        out.append("")
    if state.get("zoom") or state.get("help"):
        return out
    if state["pages"]:
        out.append(links_row(state["pages"], cols))

    # Running actions | targets and tests.
    actions = sorted(running_actions(state["progress"]), key = lambda r: -action_seconds(r))
    running, started = action_counts(state["progress"])
    running = max(len(actions), running)
    right_w = min(44, cols // 3)
    left_w = cols - right_w
    c = state["counts"]
    summary = [
        "Targets  %d configured" % c["configured"],
        "         %s%d built%s, %s%d failed%s" % (_GREEN, c["built"], _RESET, _RED if c["failed"] else "", c["failed"], _RESET),
        "Tests    %s%d passed%s, %s%d failed%s" % (_GREEN, c["tests_passed"], _RESET, _RED if c["tests_failed"] else "", c["tests_failed"], _RESET),
    ]
    cache = state.get("cache")
    if cache != None and cache[1] > 0:
        summary.append("Cache    %d%% of %d hit" % (cache[0] * 100 // cache[1], cache[1]))
    summary.append("")

    # The pane is at least 4 rows: the list gets what is left of it, and a
    # row when nothing is, in place of the spacer, then of the cache's.
    actions_list = state.get("list") == "actions"
    room = max(4, rows // 3) - len(summary)
    for _ in range(2):
        if room < 1 and len(summary) > 3 and (_items(state) or (not actions_list and state["counts"]["failures"])):
            summary = summary[:-1]
            room += 1
    summary += (action_rows if actions_list else failure_rows)(state, max(0, room), right_w - 4)

    # Clipped to the pane, the rest counted, Bazel's unlisted ones too, on
    # a row of their own.
    more = 1 if running > len(actions) else 0
    pane = max(4, min(max(len(actions) + more, len(summary)), rows // 3))
    if more or len(actions) > pane:
        listed = actions[:min(len(actions), pane - 1)]
        actions = listed + ["… and %d more" % (running - len(listed))]
    results = "Results" + (" · actions" if actions_list else "")
    if state["focus"] == "failures":
        results = ("Actions" if actions_list else "Failures") + " · ↑↓ Enter · Esc"

    # Started but not yet running (queued for a remote executor or a full
    # local pool) are counted beside the running ones, as Bazel's line has.
    head = "Running (%d of %d started)" % (running, started) if started > running else "Running (%d)" % running
    out.append(_box_top(head, left_w) + _box_top(results, right_w))
    for i in range(pane):
        a = actions[i] if i < len(actions) else ""
        s = summary[i] if i < len(summary) else ""

        # `summary` rows carry colour: pad them by their visible length.
        visible = len(_plain(s))
        s_cell = _DIM + "│ " + _RESET + s + " " * max(0, right_w - 4 - visible) + _DIM + " │" + _RESET
        if visible > right_w - 4:
            s_cell = _box_row(_plain(s), right_w)
        out.append(_box_row(a, left_w, _aged(a)) + s_cell)
    out.append(_box_bottom(left_w) + _box_bottom(right_w))

    return out

def console_height(state: dict, cols: int, rows: int) -> int:
    """How many lines the console shows at `cols` by `rows`: a page, for the
    keys that scroll by one."""
    return max(1, rows - len(_top(state, cols, rows)) - 3)

def render(state: dict, cols: int, rows: int) -> str:
    """The whole screen for `state`, `cols` by `rows`, as one string: `_top`,
    then the console pane (the console or a log, scrolled, or the keys while
    `?` shows them) and the search bar, or the keys row while it is closed,
    the keys shown or the countdown running."""
    out = _top(state, cols, rows)

    # The console, scrolled.
    view = state["view"]
    search = state["search"]
    log = shown_lines(state)
    log_h = max(1, rows - len(out) - 3)
    pattern = search["pattern"]
    scroll = 0
    if state.get("help"):
        # From the row ↑↓ have scrolled them to, a last row counting the rest
        # when they do not fit; the console's scroll left as it is.
        log = [fit(keys, 20) + " " + what for keys, what in _HELP]
        top = min(state.get("help_top", 0), max(0, len(log) - log_h))
        state["help_top"] = top
        log = log[top:]
        if len(log) > log_h:
            rest = len(log) - log_h + 1
            log = log[:log_h - 1] + ["… %d more: ↓" % rest]
        pattern = None
        scroll = max(0, len(log) - log_h)
    else:
        # Scrolled no further than the top.
        state["scroll"] = min(state["scroll"], max(0, len(log) - log_h))
        scroll = state["scroll"]
        if scroll == 0:
            state["unseen"] = 0
        if search["back"] >= len(log):
            search["back"] = -1
    end = len(log) - scroll
    end = max(min(end, len(log)), min(len(log), log_h))
    start = max(0, end - log_h)
    up = len(log) - end
    held = search["back"] >= 0 and search["pattern"] != None and search["mode"] == "jump" and view == None
    where = ("on the match, G to follow" if held else "following") if scroll == 0 else "%d line%s up" % (up, "" if up == 1 else "s")
    if state.get("unseen") and view == None:
        where += " · %d new, G to follow" % state["unseen"]

    # The pattern finding or filtering, which a query that does not compile
    # is not; a match found, which of them it is, oldest first.
    found = ""
    matches = len(log)
    current = len(log) - 1 - search["back"] if search["back"] >= 0 and pattern != None and search["mode"] == "jump" else -1
    if pattern != None and search["mode"] == "jump":
        matches = len(pattern.filter(log))
        found = " · /%s/ %s%d" % (pattern.pattern, ("%d of " % len(pattern.filter(log[:current + 1]))) if current >= 0 else "", matches)
    elif pattern != None:
        found = " · /%s/ %d" % (pattern.pattern, matches)
    name = ("Log " + view["title"] + (" (the end)" if view["cut"] else "")) if view != None else "Console"
    title = "%s%s%s · %s" % (name, _FILTER_TITLES[state["filter"]], found, where)
    if state.get("help"):
        title = "Keys · ↑↓ scroll · any other key closes"
    out.append(_box_top(title, cols))
    shown = log[start:end]
    colour = _console_row(pattern)
    for i in range(log_h):
        line = shown[i] if i < len(shown) else ""
        out.append(_box_row(line, cols, colour, start + i == current))
    out.append(_box_bottom(cols))
    if search["open"] and not state.get("help") and state.get("countdown_ms") == None:
        out.append(_search_bar(search, matches, cols))
    else:
        out.append(_DIM + keys_row(state, cols) + _RESET)

    # Home, each row cleared before it is written: cleared after, a row as
    # wide as the screen would lose its last column, where the cursor waits.
    # No newline after the last row, which would scroll.
    shown = out[:rows]
    frame = "\x1b[H" + "\r\n".join(["\x1b[K" + row for row in shown])
    if len(shown) < rows:
        frame += "\r\n\x1b[J"
    return frame

def add_lines(state: dict, lines: list):
    """Take the console's new `lines`: kept (the newest `_MAX_LOG`), errors
    noted for the scrollback, Bazel's cache tally read (`cache_hits`).
    Scrolled up, the console stays on the lines it shows, counting the new
    ones (`unseen`); a log being viewed does not move. Counted from the end,
    the current match and where the bar opened move with them, the latter
    in lines the `e` filter shows, as a search that jumps lists them."""
    shown = 0
    unsearched = 0
    for line in lines:
        state["log"].append(line)
        kind = severity(line)
        if kind == "error":
            state["errors"].append(line)
            state["error_count"] += 1
        if shown_by(state["filter"], line):
            unsearched += 1
            if console_lines(state, [line]):
                shown += 1
        cache = cache_hits(line)
        if cache != None:
            state["cache"] = cache

    if state["view"] == None:
        search = state["search"]
        held = search["back"] >= 0 and search["mode"] == "jump" and search["pattern"] != None
        if search["open"] and search.get("anchored"):
            search["origin"] += unsearched
        if state["scroll"] > 0:
            state["scroll"] += shown
            state["unseen"] += shown
        if search["back"] >= 0:
            search["back"] += shown

            # A match jumped to holds the view, even one near the newest
            # line, until `G` follows again.
            if state["scroll"] == 0 and held:
                state["scroll"] = shown
                state["unseen"] += shown
    if len(state["log"]) > _MAX_LOG:
        state["log"] = state["log"][-_MAX_LOG:]
    if len(state["errors"]) > _MAX_ERRORS:
        state["errors"] = state["errors"][-_MAX_ERRORS:]

def keys_row(state: dict, cols: int) -> str:
    """The keys row, `cols` wide: what is going on when that is all there is
    to say (cancelling, the countdown, the keys overlay open), else the keys
    for what has focus, the likeliest first, as many as fit before `? keys`."""
    if state["ended"] and state["cancelling"]:
        return fit(" Cancelled", cols)
    if state["cancelling"]:
        return fit(" cancelling… (Ctrl+C again to stop now)", cols)
    if state.get("countdown_ms") != None:
        return fit(" Done ✔ · leaving in %ds · any key stays" % ((state["countdown_ms"] + 999) // 1000), cols)
    if state.get("help"):
        return fit(" ↑↓ scroll · any other key closes", cols)
    done = state["ended"]
    items = _items(state)
    other = "a failures" if state.get("list") == "actions" else "a actions"
    has_other = bool(state.get("outputs")) or state.get("list") == "actions"
    view = state["view"]
    search = state["search"]
    parts = ["Done"] if done else []
    if state["focus"] == "failures":
        parts += ["↑↓ choose", "Enter show its log", "Tab or Esc back", "q leave" if done else "q cancel"]
        if has_other:
            parts.append(other)
    else:
        if view != None:
            parts.append("Esc back")
        if done:
            parts.append("q leave" if view != None else "q Enter Esc leave")
        else:
            parts.append("q cancel")
        if items:
            parts.append(("Tab actions" if state.get("list") == "actions" else "Tab failures") if view == None else "] [ next/prev log")
        parts.append("/ find")
        if search["pattern"] != None and search["mode"] == "jump":
            parts.append("n N older/newer match")
        parts += ["e filter", "⇧↑↓ u d scroll", "g G"]
        if has_other:
            parts.append(other)
        parts.append("z " + ("unzoom" if state.get("zoom") else "zoom"))
    text = " " + parts[0]
    for part in parts[1:]:
        if len(text) + 3 + len(part) + len(" · ? keys") > cols:
            break
        text += " · " + part
    return fit(text + " · ? keys", cols)

def on_key(state: dict, key: str, page: int) -> bool:
    """Apply a key to the dashboard's state; whether it leaves the finished
    screen. With the keys shown (`?`), ↑↓, `k`/`j` and the wheel scroll them
    and any other key closes them.
    Esc closes the innermost thing open, the search bar, the list's focus, a
    log, and on the finished screen with none open leaves, as `q` and Enter
    do. Ctrl+F or `/` opens the search bar, which takes every key but the
    arrows and the wheel (`_search_key`); `n` and `N` step through what it
    found (`step_match`). Tab moves focus to the list (unzooming, to show it)
    and back, where ↑↓ and
    the wheel choose and Enter shows a record's log in the console pane; `]`
    and `[` show the chosen one's, then the next or previous one's, from
    anywhere; `a` switches the list between failures and the actions that
    wrote output. Scroll by a line, `_WHEEL` lines (the wheel), half a `page`
    (the console's height: Shift+↑↓, `u`/`d`, Ctrl+U/D) or a `page`, never
    below the newest line; `g` goes to the oldest (`render` stops it there),
    `G` back to following (letting go of the current match). `e` moves to the next of `_FILTERS`; `z` zooms
    the console; `m` turns the mouse off and on; `q` interrupts the build,
    once, while it runs."""

    # An Esc typed just before a key reads as Alt with it: apart from the
    # bar's own Alt toggles, it is the two keys.
    if key.startswith("alt-") and not (state["search"]["open"] and key in ("alt-c", "alt-w")):
        if on_key(state, "esc", page):
            return True
        return on_key(state, key[len("alt-"):], page)
    if state.get("help"):
        if key in ("up", "k", "wheel-up"):
            state["help_top"] = max(0, state.get("help_top", 0) - 1)
        elif key in ("down", "j", "wheel-down"):
            state["help_top"] = state.get("help_top", 0) + 1
        else:
            state["help"] = False
        return False
    if state["search"]["open"] and _search_key(state, key, page):
        return False
    items = _items(state)
    pick = _pick(state)
    chosen = state.get(pick, 0)
    if key == "esc":
        if state["focus"] == "failures":
            state["focus"] = "console"
        elif state["view"] != None:
            state["view"] = None
            state["scroll"] = 0
            state["search"]["back"] = -1
        else:
            return state["ended"]
        return False
    if key == "tab":
        if items:
            state["focus"] = "console" if state["focus"] == "failures" else "failures"
            if state["focus"] == "failures":
                state["zoom"] = False
        return False
    if key == "a":
        if state.get("outputs") or state.get("list") == "actions":
            state["list"] = "failures" if state.get("list") == "actions" else "actions"
            if not _items(state):
                state["focus"] = "console"
        return False
    if state["focus"] == "failures":
        if key in ("up", "k", "wheel-up"):
            state[pick] = max(0, chosen - 1)
            return False
        if key in ("down", "j", "wheel-down"):
            state[pick] = min(len(items) - 1, chosen + 1)
            return False
        if key == "enter":
            _open_failure(state, chosen)
            return False
    if key in ("]", "[") and items:
        # The chosen record first, unless it is the one shown: then a step.
        step = 1 if key == "]" else -1
        shown = state["view"] != None and state["view"]["record"] == items[chosen]
        _open_failure(state, (chosen + step) % len(items) if shown else chosen)
        return False
    half = max(1, page // 2)
    up = {"up": 1, "k": 1, "wheel-up": _WHEEL, "shift-up": half, "u": half, "ctrl-u": half, "b": page}
    down = {"down": 1, "j": 1, "wheel-down": _WHEEL, "shift-down": half, "d": half, "ctrl-d": half, " ": page}
    if key in ("ctrl-f", "/"):
        # Where the bar opened, in the lines a search that jumps lists (a
        # kept filter's scroll counts only its matches, so the newest), no
        # higher than the top page, as `render` draws it.
        search = state["search"]
        held = search["back"] >= 0 and search["mode"] == "jump" and search["pattern"] != None and state["view"] == None
        search["open"] = True
        if search["mode"] == "filter" and search["pattern"] != None:
            search["origin"] = 0
        else:
            search["origin"] = min(state["scroll"], max(0, len(shown_lines(state)) - page))
        search["anchored"] = search["origin"] > 0 or held
    elif key in up:
        state["scroll"] += up[key]
    elif key in down:
        state["scroll"] = max(0, state["scroll"] - down[key])
    elif key == "n":
        step_match(state, -1, page)
    elif key == "N":
        step_match(state, 1, page)
    elif key == "g":
        state["scroll"] = 1 << 30
    elif key == "G":
        state["scroll"] = 0
        state["search"]["back"] = -1
    elif key == "e":
        state["filter"] = _FILTERS[(_FILTERS.index(state["filter"]) + 1) % len(_FILTERS)]
        state["scroll"] = 0
        state["search"]["back"] = -1
    elif key == "?":
        state["help"] = True
        state["help_top"] = 0
    elif key == "z":
        # Zoomed, the list is not drawn: its focus goes too.
        state["zoom"] = not state.get("zoom", False)
        if state["zoom"]:
            state["focus"] = "console"
    elif key == "m":
        if state.get("screen") != None:
            state["mouse"] = not state.get("mouse", False)
            state["screen"].mouse(state["mouse"])
    elif key == "q" or (key == "enter" and state["view"] == None):
        if state["ended"]:
            return True
        if key == "q" and not state["cancelling"]:
            state["cancelling"] = True
            state["screen"].interrupt()
    return False

def _full_screen_impl(ctx: FeatureContext):
    io = ctx.std.io
    if not (io.stdout.is_tty and io.stderr.is_tty) or ctx.std.env.var("CI") or ctx.std.term.size() == None:
        return

    state = {
        "screen": None,
        "task": "",
        "phase": "Starting Bazel…",
        "start_ms": 0,
        "now_ms": 0,
        "frames": 0,
        "progress": [],
        "log": [],
        "errors": [],
        "scroll": 0,
        "filter": "all",
        "search": new_search(),
        "re": None,
        "read": None,
        "ansi": None,
        "controls": None,
        "env": None,
        "fails": [],
        "outputs": [],
        "list": "failures",
        "selected": 0,
        "picked": 0,
        "focus": "console",
        "view": None,
        "help": False,
        "zoom": False,
        "mouse": ctx.args.mouse,
        "unseen": 0,
        "cache": None,
        "countdown_ms": None,
        "exec_root": "",
        "output_dirs": {},
        "test_counts": {},
        "last_attempts": {},
        "spread": {},
        "logged_index": None,
        "cancelling": False,
        "ended": False,
        "counts": new_counts(),
        "error_count": 0,
        "fresh": False,
        "log_path": "",
        "pages": [],
        "bes_base": "",
        "bazel_uuid": "",
        "update": {},
    }

    def _add_lines(lines):
        add_lines(state, lines)

    def _frame(task_ctx, build):
        # A build whose console is shown (its own `output =`) is left alone.
        feed = build.output_feed()
        if feed == None:
            return

        # The runtime gives a screen back on a hook's error.
        if state["screen"] != None and not state["screen"].held:
            state["screen"] = None
        if state["screen"] == None:
            if task_ctx.std.term.taken:
                return
            state["screen"] = task_ctx.std.term.take()

            # The user's `m` outlives a screen the runtime took back.
            if state["mouse"]:
                state["screen"].mouse(True)
            state["re"] = task_ctx.std.re
            state["read"] = task_ctx.std.fs.try_read_tail
            state["ansi"] = task_ctx.std.re.compile(ANSI)
            state["controls"] = task_ctx.std.re.compile(CONTROLS)
            state["env"] = task_ctx.std.env
            state["task"] = task_ctx.task.kind
            state["start_ms"] = int(monotonic() * 1000)
        if state["fresh"]:
            reset_attempt(state, int(monotonic() * 1000))
        state["ended"] = False
        state["pages"] = results_pages(state["bes_base"], state["bazel_uuid"], state["update"])
        screen = state["screen"]
        state["now_ms"] = int(monotonic() * 1000)
        state["frames"] += 1
        _add_lines(feed["lines"])

        # A frame that caught Bazel between drawing its progress area and
        # redrawing it keeps the last one, which the header shows too.
        if feed["progress"]:
            state["progress"] = feed["progress"]
            state["phase"] = feed["progress"][0]
        _add_lines(["» " + l for l in screen.output()])
        cols, rows = screen.size()
        page = console_height(state, cols, rows)
        for _ in range(16):
            key = screen.key()
            if key == None:
                break
            on_key(state, key, page)
        screen.write(render(state, cols, rows))

    def _event(task_ctx, event):
        kind = event.kind
        if kind == "build_started":
            state["bazel_uuid"] = str(_get(event.payload, "uuid", "") or "")
        elif kind == "options_parsed":
            state["bes_base"] = bes_results_url(event) or state["bes_base"]
        event_id, payload = _get(event, "id"), _get(event, "payload")
        count_event(state["counts"], kind, event_id, payload)
        note_event(state, kind, event_id, payload, type(payload) == "aborted")

    def _update(task_ctx, update):
        if update.data:
            state["update"] = update.data

    def _attempt_end(task_ctx, exit_code):
        state["fresh"] = True

    def _end(task_ctx, build):
        state["log_path"] = build.output_log or ""
        screen = state["screen"]
        if screen == None or not screen.held:
            return
        state["ended"] = True
        state["cancelling"] = state["cancelling"] or task_ctx.cancellation.root.cancelled
        state["progress"] = []
        state["phase"] = "Done"
        cols, rows = screen.size()
        screen.write(render(state, cols, rows))

        # A task that goes on after its build (`lint`'s reports, `warming`'s
        # uploads) gets the terminal back now; only `build` and `test` end
        # with their build, and keep the screen up after.
        if task_ctx.task.kind not in _STAY_KINDS:
            state["screen"] = None
            screen.release()
            _report(task_ctx)

    def _stay(task_ctx, screen, countdown_ms):
        """Keep the finished screen up, scrollable, until a leave key, or
        until the run is told to stop (a SIGTERM), which no key would end.
        With a `countdown_ms`, it leaves by itself once that has gone by,
        unless a key comes first, which only stops it. The screen is the state's meanwhile, for
        the keys that use it (`m`)."""
        state["screen"] = screen
        _hold(task_ctx, screen, countdown_ms)
        state["screen"] = None

    def _hold(task_ctx, screen, countdown_ms):
        screen.interrupts(False)
        deadline = int(monotonic() * 1000) + countdown_ms if countdown_ms else None
        for _ in range(1 << 30):
            if task_ctx.cancellation.root.cancelled or not screen.held:
                return
            if deadline != None:
                state["countdown_ms"] = max(0, deadline - int(monotonic() * 1000))
                if state["countdown_ms"] == 0:
                    return
            _add_lines(["» " + l for l in screen.output()])
            cols, rows = screen.size()
            screen.write(render(state, cols, rows))
            key = screen.key(timeout_ms = 100)
            if key == "ctrl-c":
                return
            if key != None and deadline != None:
                # The key that stops the countdown does nothing else: Enter
                # or Esc there means to stay, not to leave sooner.
                deadline = None
                state["countdown_ms"] = None
            elif key != None and on_key(state, key, console_height(state, cols, rows)):
                return

    def _post(task_ctx, outcome):
        screen = state["screen"]
        if screen == None:
            return
        state["screen"] = None
        if screen.held:
            # Not for a cancelled task (`q`, or Ctrl+C): the CLI is on its way
            # out, and would force the exit before a key came.
            if task_ctx.cancellation.root.cancelled:
                state["cancelling"] = True
            if ctx.args.stay != "never" and task_ctx.std.io.stdin.is_tty and not state["cancelling"]:
                state["ended"] = True
                state["progress"] = []
                state["phase"] = "Done"
                c = state["counts"]
                passed = outcome.exit_code == 0 and not state["fails"] and not c["failures"] and not c["tests_failed"]
                _stay(task_ctx, screen, _COUNTDOWN_MS if passed and ctx.args.stay == "failure" else 0)
            screen.release()
        _report(task_ctx)

    def _report(task_ctx):
        """Bazel's errors, each failure (with its log's path when it reads, then
        the failed targets with none), the results pages and the counts, to
        the scrollback once the screen is gone."""
        err = task_ctx.std.io.stderr
        errors = state["errors"]
        if errors:
            dropped = state["error_count"] - len(errors)
            err.write(_BOLD + "Bazel's errors" + _RESET + (" (the last %d of %d)" % (len(errors), state["error_count"]) if dropped else "") + ":\n")
            for line in errors:
                err.write("  " + _RED + line + _RESET + "\n")
        index = logged_index(state)
        unlogged = [label for label in index["unlogged"] if label not in index["logged"]]
        if state["fails"] or unlogged:
            err.write(_BOLD + "Failed" + _RESET + ":\n")
            for rec in state["fails"]:
                # A path that reads: one never downloaded is no use here.
                paths = [p for _, v in rec["files"] for p in log_paths(state, rec, v) if state["read"](p, 0) != None]
                err.write("  %s✗%s %s  %s%s\n" % (_RED, _RESET, rec["label"], failure_detail(state, rec), ("  " + paths[0]) if paths else ""))
            for label in unlogged:
                err.write("  %s✗%s %s\n" % (_RED, _RESET, label))
        for label, url in results_pages(state["bes_base"], state["bazel_uuid"], state["update"]):
            err.write("%s: %s\n" % (label, link(url, url) if task_ctx.std.io.stderr.is_tty else url))
        c = state["counts"]
        err.write("%d built, %d failed · %d tests passed, %d failed%s\n" % (
            c["built"],
            c["failed"],
            c["tests_passed"],
            c["tests_failed"],
            (" · Bazel's console: " + state["log_path"]) if state["log_path"] else "",
        ))

    t = ctx.traits[BazelTrait]
    t.output_display = "none"
    if ctx.args.actions == "all" and _PUBLISH_ALL_ACTIONS not in t.extra_flags:
        t.extra_flags.append(_PUBLISH_ALL_ACTIONS)

    # A processor needs a stage; the console's defaults may be off.
    t.output_stages.append(bazel.output.observe({"full-screen:error": r"^ERROR:"}))
    t.output_frame.append(_frame)
    t.output_end.append(_end)
    t.build_event.append(_event)
    t.bazel_attempt_end.append(_attempt_end)
    ctx.traits[Phases].task_update.append(_update)
    ctx.hooks.post_task(_post)

FullScreen = feature(
    display_name = "Full screen",
    summary = "Replace Bazel's terminal UI with a full-screen dashboard of running actions, results and a scrollable console.",
    implementation = _full_screen_impl,
    enabled = False,
    args = {
        "stay": args.string(
            default = "failure",
            values = ["failure", "always", "never"],
            bare = "always",
            description = "Whether the screen stays up once the task ends, until q, Enter, Esc or Ctrl+C: `failure` (the default) stays when anything failed, and with nothing failed counts down 3 seconds first, which any key stops; `always` stays; `never` leaves at once. Only when stdin is a terminal.",
        ),
        "mouse": args.boolean(
            default = True,
            description = "Scroll with the mouse wheel. The terminal then needs a modifier to select text (Shift, Option or Fn with the drag); `m` turns it off and on while the screen is up.",
        ),
        "actions": args.string(
            default = "failed",
            values = ["failed", "all"],
            description = "Which actions' logs the screen can show: `failed`, or `all` that wrote any (`a` lists them), adding --build_event_publish_all_actions so Bazel reports them.",
        ),
    },
)
